  "analytic_review_groups": [
    "Freezing a smooth background gives an arbitrarily small positive correction; local bounded truncations and plurifine locality prove restricted mixed positivity.",
    "Polynomial dependence on the correction has coefficients of locally finite variation, allowing total-variation limits after restriction to a fixed Borel set.",
    "Compatible positive truncations give a sigma-finite Borel measure with zero pluripolar mass; smooth changes of decomposition preserve it.",
    "The ordered contact inequality is proved on common finite levels before passing to the increasing exhaustion.",
    "Weighted lower semicontinuity uses a continuous cutoff vanishing at its truncation level, bounded mixed capacity estimates and quasi-continuous weights; infinite total mass is allowed.",
    "The convex density inequality retains its smooth semipositive-background hypothesis so global truncations remain positive.",
    "The full trace lower bound passes by distributional convergence and integrable root densities; pole-set trace mass is retained.",
    "Metric comparison and exact bounded regularization give BMP metric independence and the same supremum over smooth potentials.",
    "BMP uniformly bounds every extended measure below a controlled background; its converse is supplied by the explicit infinite-mass witness.",
    "Dyadic positive combinations have bounded L1 norm and mass above each a_j^n M_j; the smooth summand gives no mass to the pole set.",
    "Jensen with unused tail weight at zero gives exponential integrability at every exponent, hence trivial multiplier ideals.",
    "Positive coefficient series converge by the Gauduchon trace identity; the coefficient L1 error is exactly bounded by twice the tail mass.",
    "Bounded tail truncations dominate every finite smooth partial determinant; matrix monotonicity then gives the determinant lower bound and infinite integral.",
    "Positive rescaling yields arbitrarily small potential and coefficient L1 distances and trace total variation while both nonlinear masses remain infinite.",
    "Prescribed smooth masses use the intermediate value theorem for a continuous polynomial; no monotonicity of that polynomial is assumed.",
    "Convex exponential estimates give all fixed-exponent L1 convergence of the prescribed smooth sequence.",
    "The chapter opening, all seven sections and history seam are coherent; the later addition and normalization uses retain weighted cutoff and semipositivity hypotheses."
  ],
